In bug bounty programs, companies do not pay for the use of comments specifically; rather, they pay for the severity and business impact of the vulnerability that the comment helps uncover or execute. [1] 
The industry follows standard classification models—such as the Bugcrowd Vulnerability Rating Taxonomy (VRT) or HackerOne’s Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS)—to determine monetary payouts. [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] 
------------------------------
## Vulnerability Payouts Involving Code Comments
The following matrix outlines the vulnerabilities where code comments play a critical role, categorized by standard severity tiers and industry-average payouts. [7, 8] 

| Vulnerability Type | How Comments are Used | Industry Severity Tier | Average Payout Range |
|---|---|---|---|
| SQL Injection (SQLi) | Using comment characters (--, /* */, #) to cut off back-end logic or bypass WAFs. | P1 - Critical | $3,500 – $15,000+ |
| Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) | Using // or <!-- --> to neutralize code breaks or bypass length sanitization filters. | P3 to P2 - Medium/High | $500 – $3,000 |
| Information Disclosure | Finding hardcoded API keys, database passwords, or private internal URLs left in public comments. | P4 to P2 - Low/High | $200 – $5,000+ (Depends on leak sensitivity) |
| Business Logic Flaws | Reviewing // TODO: or // FIXME: development notes to find unpatched logic holes or hidden parameters. | P3 to P1 - Variable | $1,000 – $10,000+ |
| Source Code Disclosure | Finding backup or commented-out configuration scripts left in production files. | P3 - Medium | $500 – $1,500 |

------------------------------
## Key Factors That Dictate Your Payout

   1. The Target Company Profile:
   * Big Tech (like the [Google VRP](https://bughunters.google.com/) or [GitHub Bug Bounty](https://bounty.github.com/)) pays drastically higher rewards, scaling up to $30,000 to $250,000 for critical exploits.
      * Mid-sized companies or newer public programs usually start their payouts much lower, often capping at $3,500 for high-priority bugs. [2, 9, 10, 11] 
   2. The "Impact" Factor:
   * Finding a commented-out string that says // TODO: fix login bypass pays $0 by itself.
      * If you use that note to actually execute an authentication bypass, it is evaluated as a Critical (P1) finding, yielding the maximum platform payout. [2, 12, 13] 
   3. WAF Bypasses via Comments:
   * Simply proving you bypassed a web application firewall using SQL comments (SEL/*comment*/ECT) without extracting data is usually marked as Low (P4) severity.
      * To get a full payout, you must chain the bypass to exfiltrate database data. [2, 14] 
   
